  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nCooler dewpoints (readings in the upper 60s to lower 70s)\ncontinue to filter in leading to a relatively pleasant day across\nmuch of the Mid-South. However, portions of eastern Arkansas and\nwestern north Mississippi, where pesky mid 70 dewpoints remain,\nare under a Heat Advisory with heat indices up to 109F expected\nthrough 8PM. Shower and thunderstorm chances will also begin to\nincrease and spread across our area today as a warm front,\ncurrently extending from Monroe County Mississippi through north\ncentral Arkansas, lifts north.\n\nThe latest KNQA radar sweep indicates a line of showers with a\nfew rumbles of thunder over northeastern Arkansas and the\nMissouri Bootheel. As we edge into the afternoon hours, overall\ncoverage of showers and thunderstorms will begin to slowly\nincrease. A window for a few strong to severe thunderstorms will\nopen up as a 30-40 kt low-level jet begins to kick in. As this\njet kicks in, forecast soundings indicate around 1700 J/kg of\nSBCAPE, 20-25 kts of effective shear, DCAPE of around 500, and\nmid, low-level lapse rates around 5.8 C/km, and slightly curved\nhodographs. This environment may support a few strong to severe\nthunderstorms that could have enough upscale growth to produce\ndamaging wind gusts and potentially a brief spin up tornado\nthrough the early evening hours. Moving closer to sunset, a\nslightly positively titled trough, currently over the Midwest,\nwill begin to push east into the Upper-Mississippi Valley. This\ntrough will also eject a cold front across the Mid-South this\nevening. Scattered to numerous shower and thunderstorm chances\nwill form ahead of this front as PWATs begin to surge to near the\nclimatological maximum for this time of year, 2.2-2.5\". As we\nmove a few hours past sunset, convective inhibition looks to\nincrease, likely cutting off our window for severe thunderstorms.\nThough, with surging PWATs we may transition into more of a heavy\nrainfall threat as we head into the late evening to overnight\nhours. Isolated to scattered showers and a few thunderstorms,\ngreatest thunder potential along the Tennessee River, will linger\ninto the day tomorrow as the aforementioned trough pushes into\nthe southern Ohio River Valley. Though, largely pleasant\nconditions will fall across the Mid-South Saturday with partly\ncloudy skies and temperatures ranging from the low 80s to low\n90s.\n\nBehind the aforementioned cold front, it will feel more like late\nSeptember as opposed to early August with highs in the upper 70s\nto mid 80s Sunday and Monday morning lows in the mid 60s to low\n70s. Isolated to scattered shower chances will also exist on\nSunday mainly over West Tennessee, the area closest to the\naforementioned trough. Monday will be a near rinse and repeat of\nSunday, just a tad warmer as nearly north flow builds in aloft.\nPushing past Monday, we're looking largely dry and will begin a\nwarming trend through late week as upper-level ridging begins to\nbroaden east. Though dewpoints aren't expected to surge into the\nupper 70s, they could teeter into the mid 70s, potentially\nwarranting Heat Advisories as early as Wednesday. Enjoy the\ncooler start to August while it lasts.\n\nAEH",
